  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we consider the expressions inside each of the parentheses. The first one is.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}\\times {(-5)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}\\times {(-5)}^{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\times 25 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{25}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\times 25}{5\\times 1} & = \\\\ \\frac{50}{5} & = \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression inside the second parentheses is.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-2)}^{5}-(-20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-2)}^{5}-(-20) & = \\\\ (-32)+20 & = \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{5}\\times {(-5)}^{2})÷({(-2)}^{5}-(-20)) & = \\\\ 10÷(-12) & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{(-12)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{10}{12} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
